| Servo      | ESP32 GPIO  | Servo Wire             |
| ---------- | ----------- | ---------------------- |
| Base       | GPIO 14     | Signal (Orange/Yellow) |
| Shoulder   | GPIO 16     | Signal (Orange/Yellow) |
| Elbow      | GPIO 17     | Signal (Orange/Yellow) |
| Gripper    | GPIO 18     | Signal (Orange/Yellow) |
| All Servos | External 5V | Red (VCC)              |
| All Servos | Common GND  | Brown/Black (GND)      |

## ⚠️ Important Connection Notes

- Use an **external regulated 5V power supply (3A–5A recommended)** to power all servo motors.
- **Do NOT power the servos directly from the ESP32's 5V pin**, as the board cannot supply enough current for multiple servos.
- Connect the **GND (Ground)** of the external power supply to the **ESP32 GND**. A common ground is required for reliable servo control.
- Connect only the **servo signal wires** to the ESP32 GPIO pins:
  - GPIO 14 → Base Servo
  - GPIO 16 → Shoulder Servo
  - GPIO 17 → Elbow Servo
  - GPIO 18 → Gripper Servo
- Ensure the power supply can handle the total current required when multiple servos move simultaneously.
- Keep power wires short and use adequately sized wires to reduce voltage drops.
- Verify all wiring before powering the circuit to avoid damaging the ESP32 or servos.
- If using high-torque servos (e.g., MG996R or MG995), consider using a **5V 5A** power supply.
- Never connect a servo directly to the ESP32 without a shared ground connection.
- Check servo movement limits in software to prevent mechanical stress or damage to the robot arm.
